doctype html

- var title = "Implementing reassigned spectrogram"
- var datestart = "20250814"
- var dateend = "20251129"

head
	title #{title}
	<link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="general.css">
	<script id="MathJax-script" async src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@4/tex-mml-chtml.js"></script>
body
	div
		h1 #{title}
		p Started #{datestart}
		p Continued #{dateend}
		p 
			a(href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.3080") Time-frequency reassignment
			|  is a method of correcting the vertical and horizontal positions of points on a 
			| time-frequency grid of a generic short-time Fourier transform. 
			| In simple words, on a reassigned spectrogram you will see a straight line from 
			| a pure tone, instead of a «sausage» where center of it is a desired frequency.
		p 
			| You can find it in some quality spectrum analyzers. For example 
			a(href="https://www.image-line.com/fl-studio-learning/fl-studio-online-manual/html/plugins/Wave%20Candy.htm") Wave Candy
			|  in FL Studio uses it in it's «Enhanced frequency» mode. Also 
			a(href="https://www.tritik.com/product/visu/") Visu
			|  and 
			a(href="https://minimeters.app/") MiniMeters
			|  are using this method to enhance resolution.
			| Though I think Wave Candy uses the phase information directly to do it's corrections 
			| (like a phase vocoder), which does not require even using more than one FFT.
		p 
			| The method itself is really simple. You just use two more FFTs with two different windows
			| on an original audio frame you took for an analysis, do some complex divisions and
			| there you are. The formulas in Fitz & Fullop are needlessly expanded from their 
			| simple complex forms, and if your language supports complex math you can just use
			| them directly from the referenced 
			a(href="https://hal.science/hal-00414583/document") Auger & Flandrin paper
			| :
		p $$\delta_t = -\mathrm{Im}\left({X_d \over X}\right) $$
		p $$\delta_\omega = \mathrm{Re}\left({X_t \over X}\right) $$
		p 
			| Except in practice, time displacement must be divided by 2π, and multiplier in time window 
			| \(\left(X_t\right)\) is given in samples if you're multiplied the time ramp by nfft as 
			| described in papers. 
			| You can also multiply the time ramp by overlap factor instead of nfft and 
			| the reassigned time will be in frames.
		ul
			li 
				| Time displacement must be divided by 2×pi. 
				| If you haven't got it working, that's why.
			li 
				| Multiplier in time window is the time scale. 
				| If you're using window size it will be in samples. 
				| You can also use overlap factor so reassigned time scale will be in frames.
			li 
				| Formulas for implementing TFR with “flattened” complex math.
			li 
				| TFR is invertible if you are using it to derive some mask. 
				| Just remember original bin time-frequency coordinates for each point. 
				| This property can be used for source separation purposes, for example. 
				| One probably can derive a HTDemucs-like architecture, 
				| but instead of double time-frequency domain processing make it «glow» 
				| only certain points for each of the source types.
			li 
				| Example of de-speckling the spectrum with inversion in C++.
